# TP4 mixed P/D profile: matched work, no kernel-launch-count reduction

Both frozen v1 capture audits and the raw/group matched-work comparison passed.
Grouping P did **not** reduce the exported native kernel-launch count in this
capture: each rank recorded 2,854 raw versus 2,924 group
`AscendCL@aclrtLaunchKernelWithHostArgs` calls. The separately measured +3.466%
group/raw mixed-work throughput increment therefore cannot be attributed to
fewer such launches. This profile is a diagnostic, not another throughput test.

The runs are `profile-f7-raw-mixed-01` and `profile-f7-group-mixed-01`, with the
frozen f7/Core/Ascend sources, BF16 Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, TP4/EP4, HCCL=true and the
matching fusion backend. Raw/group differ only in the grouped-P option and the
profiler destination. Both controllers exited zero, completed collection and
returned from shutdown. Root separately confirmed each owned PID/process group
absent and the four physical NPUs 1/2/3/6 empty. Native internal cleanup still
used SIGTERM for four workers in each run; this is not a claim of signal-free
graceful shutdown.

## Actual observed capture

After one complete unprofiled mixed warmup, four P128/D512 seeds each returned
32 tokens. Profiling then covered P12000 admission through its first token.
Both roles observed the same ten steps, 33–42: P advanced and each seed returned
one D token at every step. Each rank's D-controller replay delta and exported
`AscendCL@aclmdlRIExecuteAsync` count were both exactly ten. Group recorded one
successful call to each of ten P groups on every rank; raw had no grouped-P
executor. There were no capture/fallback/uncaptured/forward-failure deltas.

Each of the eight traces has one profiling-enable and one profiling-disable
marker, no graph-capture API, a native profiler rank/PID binding, and a distinct
observed hardware label. The workers reported profilers stopped before the
remaining unprofiled output completion. These checks passed in the original,
unchanged auditor. Visible ordinals are not substituted for host physical IDs.

The five complete output sequences in both warmup and capture completion match
between raw and group, including finish states: all ten retained comparisons
pass in [the token-array comparison](retained-profile-token-comparison-v1.json).
This does not establish native-relative answer quality.

## Runtime calls

Counts below hold separately on **each** of the four ranks, except the explicitly
listed query rows. They describe the entire captured mixed window, not isolated
P kernel work. All 364 rank/API rows, including unchanged calls and inclusive
host durations, are retained in [the complete table](all-runtime-api-comparisons-v1.csv)
and [the frozen comparison output](comparison-v1.json).

| Exported API | Raw count/rank | Group count/rank | Change/rank |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `aclmdlRIExecuteAsync` | 10 | 10 | 0 |
| `aclrtLaunchKernelWithHostArgs` | 2854 | 2924 | +70 |
| `aclnnInplaceCopy` | 909 | 1039 | +130 |
| `aclnnInplaceCopyGetWorkspaceSize` | 115 | 175 | +60 |
| `aclrtMemcpyAsync` | 303 | 363 | +60 |
| `aclrtRecordEvent` | 462 | 462 | 0 |
| `aclrtStreamWaitEvent` | 346 | 346 | 0 |
| `Event::record` | 183 | 183 | 0 |
| `Event::wait` | 162 | 162 | 0 |
| `Event::synchronize` | 11 | 11 | 0 |

`aclrtQueryEventStatus` counts vary by rank: raw [85,85,3,3] and group
[26,27,27,27]. The full inventory retains these differences. Readiness-sensitive
query and destroy counts are not a controlled, universal reduction. Inclusive
API durations include waiting and can overlap; summing them across ranks is not
end-to-end time. Profiling perturbs execution, and these single captures do not
estimate the significance or cause of the separate timing result. No P-only
device-time, physical P/D overlap or Cube/Vector partition claim follows.

## Parsing and evidence preservation

Both native worker logs report that the profiler cannot parse data inside a
daemon process. Collection nevertheless completed with raw profiling data on
all ranks. Root used the installed torch-npu 2.10.0.post4 offline `analyse`
interface with `max_process_number=1`, after all model processes stopped.
The script copied the profiler directories into a new analysis tree, verified
copy identities before parsing, and verified every original member unchanged
afterward. It produced four complete traces per role. The original error logs,
raw directories, complete copied/parsed tree, parser script/log/receipt and
terminal device checks are preserved; no original capture was rewritten.

`root/audit_mixed_profiles_v1.py` maps each copied trace by the native
`profiler_info_*.json` rank ID and invokes the unchanged frozen auditor. All
three CLI calls exited zero; [command receipts](command-receipts-v1.json) bind
the wrapper, auditor and parser receipt. [Raw](raw-capture-audit-v1.json) and
[group](group-capture-audit-v1.json) audits retain every trace hash and observed
counter. No API alias or acceptance condition was revised after seeing data.

The no-profiler performance matrix and its limitations remain in the separate
[canonical v4 report](https://github.com/vLLM-HUST/vllm-hust-benchmark/tree/ef849ef49577c491fee68317ffc96d52796cd379/reports/muxpd-followup-20261025/performance-lifecycle-v4).
The current quality failure remains separate: group preserves the measured raw
behavior but neither candidate passed the frozen native-relative answer gate.
